    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Nzinga took power in Ndongo in 1624 and fought Portuguese expansion for some thirty years, first in exile, then from the kingdom of Matamba, which she conquered between 1631 and 1635.</li><li>She was a diplomat as much as a soldier: she negotiated with the Portuguese, allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imbangala">Imbangala</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, and in 1656 made a peace that recognised her rule and gave her a monopoly of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>.</li><li>Her wars were financed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> she expanded, and historians such as Linda Heywood caution against portraying her as either a populist hero or a tyrant.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Her name is said to come from a difficult birth: the umbilical cord was wrapped around her neck, and the Kimbundu verb kujinga means to twist or turn.</p></aside>
    </section>

